.TH FSLEDGER.YAML 5 "2026-10-22" "fsledger" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
fsledger.yaml \- fsledger configuration
.SH DESCRIPTION
The default file is /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml. Use -c to select another file.
Each file contains one YAML document, with strict keys and no duplicates.
Source and state paths must be clean and absolute. Restart to apply changes;
only the on-disk meaning of repository paths is re-read while running.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.B server.name
Host identity used in Git messages. Default: localhost.
.TP
.B storage.path
Private repository root. Default: /var/lib/fsledger/repos.
.TP
.B storage.git.timeout
Positive Go duration limiting each bulk Git command, including hooks and
foreground automatic maintenance. Default: 10m. May be overridden per
repository.
rev-parse queries retain a 30-second limit. Timeout cancels the Git process
group;
the direct process is also killed. Hooks that deliberately detach into another
session are outside that group. Waiting for inherited pipes is bounded by a
one-second WaitDelay; this does not guarantee termination of detached
descendants.
Git lock files left by a cancelled command are removed then, and whenever the
daemon, verify or outbox clear opens the repository; no other Git process may
write to the mirror.
Partial failures require unknown-actor reconciliation before further commits.
.TP
.B runtime
Private volatile-state directory for status snapshots and flush requests.
Default: /run/fsledger. Status replacement is atomic but is not synchronized for
durability across machine restarts; catalogs and pending decisions use
storage.path.
.TP
.B logging.level
Minimum severity: debug, info, warning or error. Default: info.
The command-line --debug flag overrides this value for daemon execution.
.TP
.B logging.file
Optional absolute log file path; empty by default, selecting standard error.
A configured file replaces standard error for daemon logs and is opened in
append mode. Missing parent directories are created with mode 0700 and new
files with mode 0600, subject to umask. Existing file permissions are retained.
The destination must be a regular file. Symlinks, special files and symlink
parents are rejected. Overlap with storage, runtime or a literal source path is
a configuration error. A pattern match, or a source whose symlinked parent
resolves onto the log file, is skipped in its entirety and reported in
skipped_sources.
An open failure aborts startup. Check only inspects the path and creates
nothing.
There is no built-in rotation. Restart after renaming a log to reopen its path;
SIGHUP does not reopen it. Errors before logging is initialized use stderr.
.TP
.B watch.backend
One of auto, fanotify, inotify or polling. Default: auto.
Capability failures fall back to supported detection.
.TP
.B watch.reconcile.enabled
Periodic reconciliation, default true. Remote filesystems may force it on, as
may a filesystem mounted below a source after startup, which neither fanotify
nor inotify follows; inotify reconciles the directory when it is mounted or
unmounted. So do directories beyond fanotify's per-user mark limit
(fs.fanotify.max_user_marks), which applies when the daemon lacks
CAP_SYS_ADMIN; status then carries an advisory.
.TP
.B watch.reconcile.interval
Positive Go duration, default 5m. Example units: ms, s, m, h.
The next interval begins after reconciliation finishes, preventing continuously
queued scans when a traversal takes longer than the interval.
.TP
.B watch.reconcile.schedule, watch.reconcile.timezone
Optional five-field cron expression replacing interval; timezone defaults to
UTC.
Explicit schedule replaces the inherited interval and explicit interval replaces
the inherited schedule. Specifying both in the same document is an error.
The existing scanner traverses the whole selected repository and reuses its
catalog and concurrency budget. Coincident ordinary and full-hash deadlines
produce one full scan. Jobs do not overlap; missed cron occurrences are skipped.
.TP
.B watch.reconcile.on_start, watch.reconcile.on_stop
Both default to true. Only explicit polling permits false, deferring startup
inventory or omitting shutdown reconciliation. An empty catalog records nothing,
and so reports no change, until its first complete scan. Polling with any scan
cron retries failed
or unstable observations at the next configured slot instead of continuously
rescanning outside its window. Explicit administrative operations remain
possible.
.TP
.B attribution.audit.enabled
Enable the passive Audit subscriber, default true. Does not enable kernel
Audit.
.TP
.B attribution.audit.key
Key of administrator-supplied Audit rules, default fsledger. When enabled,
requires 1-31 letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens.
.TP
.B commit.debounce
Quiet interval before committing a group, default 2s; must be positive.
.TP
.B commit.max_delay
Maximum group delay, default 30s; must be at least debounce.
.TP
.B commit.grouping.by_process
Group by observed PID and verified start time, default true.
.TP
.B commit.grouping.by_user
Separate observed user identities, default true.
.TP
.B commit.grouping.prefer_login_uid
Prefer a known login UID for grouping, default true.
.TP
.B paths.repositories, paths.notifiers, paths.excludes
Lists of files or directories containing named objects. A directory loads its
immediate .yaml/.yml files in sorted order. Explicit files may have other
suffixes.
The filename minus .yaml/.yml supplies the name; do not repeat it inside the
file.
Relative catalog paths resolve from the main configuration. Repeated files,
duplicate names, missing files and invalid YAML are errors. Maximum 256 files
and 4 MiB including templates. Files must be regular.
.TP
.B paths.templates
YAML file or directory of immediate .yaml/.yml files. The filename minus its
YAML extension identifies the template. Each document requires subject and body
text/template strings; unknown keys and the former plain-text format are
rejected. check and daemon startup render the template of each notifier with
sample messages of every event a repository routes to it, including a change
above 250 paths; a template that fails, such as an unknown field, an index out
of range, Report outside reports or a subject with a line break, is rejected.
.TP
.B scan.workers
Global concurrent filesystem work limit, default 2, shared across repositories.
Applies to observations, hashes and mirror copies; not an OS thread limit.
Budgets are shared within each daemon process, not across separate services.
.TP
.B scan.max_concurrent
Maximum active catalog scans per process, default 2, range 1 to 256.
Waiting for admission is cancellable and does not reserve an observation worker.
The first ordinary interval starts at a repository-specific offset if startup
observation is deferred. A completed scan resets its interval from completion.
Startup scans, cron occurrences and the full-hash timer are not staggered.
.TP
.B paths
Repository file setting: sources to mirror. Entries that do not exist are
skipped
without error, so one list may serve hosts with different software; check
reports
how many are absent and status lists them as missing_sources. An entry may be a
pattern: *, ? and [...] match within one component, including names starting
with
a dot. ** and {} are rejected, as is a pattern in the first directory; escape a
literal metacharacter with a backslash. A symlinked parent directory is replaced
by its target when the link belongs to root or to the daemon user, so
/usr/src/linux/.config is mirrored below the real kernel directory; the final
component is never followed. Matches that resolve into state directories, onto
the log file, into a path Git remote or onto a directory containing one, or into
another repository of the same type are skipped whole and reported
as skipped_sources. A path remote named through symlinks is compared as its
trusted links resolve, like a source. Special files cannot be selection roots; their metadata may
still be observed beneath a selected directory.
The selection is re-read at ordinary or full-hash scan deadlines, whether
configured
by interval or cron, and on flush for Git repositories: new matches are
adopted and
deleted ones recorded as deletions without
restarting. A selected source whose filesystem disappeared is kept, listed as
unavailable_sources, and nothing is pruned until it returns. The filesystem
mounted again at the mount point that held the source returns it, whatever its
mount ID, when it is the same one: same type and root, and same fsid, or same
mount source for a network filesystem. Another filesystem mounted there, or one
stacked over it, does not. A filesystem
mounted inside a source is recorded too: once unmounted, its tree is kept and
listed as unmounted_paths, and scans count as partial, until it is mounted
again or leaves the selection. The catalog keeps these mount points across
restarts: a source found on one that is no longer mounted, or missing below
it, stays unavailable instead of being recorded as an empty tree, and verify
fails meanwhile. To retire a mount point for good, take its sources out of the
selection or exclude it. An entry or pattern that cannot be read (permission denied,
I/O error, stale handle, too many symlinks) is listed as skipped_sources but
proves no deletion: the daemon keeps every source it watched and, after a
restart, every tree its catalog recorded outside the selection, until the entry
can be read again. verify refuses to run meanwhile. Two repositories of one type
may not select the same tree; a git and a db
repository may, since one keeps content and the other verifies it. Conflicting
matches are assigned to the first repository in name order and skipped in the
others. Inside one
repository a pattern may fall below another entry, and the wider one is watched.
.TP
.B type
Repository file setting: git (default) or db. DB stores hashes and metadata,
not file contents. Repository files may override commit, watch, attribution,
storage, integrity, notifications and reports. Omitted fields inherit global
defaults; explicit
false and empty lists override them. No Git/DB conversion is performed.
.TP
.B Catalog storage
The catalog uses Pebble, using Protobuf records and Snappy compression without
CGO.
Catalog directories require local storage. No permanent observation history is
kept.
.TP
.B Integrity policy
Each observation is compared with the previous observation of the same path.
The compared fields are hash, type, target, mode, uid and gid, plus xattrs and
acl when integrity.xattrs and integrity.acl select them. A difference in any of
them is a change; other recorded attributes (size, ctime, atime, btime, inode,
device, nlink, and xattrs and acl unless selected) are kept in the inventory but
never produce a change on their own. ctime is metadata change time. btime
availability is stored explicitly.
mtime is a change only when it moves more than one hour before the btime of
the same file, as touch \-d or utimensat leave it; such a change is coded T.
A recreated file has a new btime, so a copy that replaces a file and keeps its
time (tar, rsync \-a, unzip) is reported only when its content or another
compared field differs. Restoring a time in place keeps the btime and is
reported: cp \-p over an existing file, and tar or rsync \-a on directories
that already exist (unless integrity.directories is false).
Alerts and reports mark any change of a version whose mtime predates its btime
(Detail.BackdatedBy).
The first scan of an empty catalog records every entry as created and, when
change alerts or reports are enabled, announces all of them. To build it
silently, run verify with the daemon stopped while the repository has
notifications.use: [] and reports.enabled: false, then restore them.
Attributes the filesystem does not provide are not recorded: atime only where
the mount keeps access times current (not noatime or relatime, and not
nodiratime for directories), btime only where statx returns it, and xattrs and
ACLs only where they are supported. Such attributes are neither stored nor
shown.
xattrs and ACLs too large to observe (over 1 MiB of names and values, or more
than 64 KiB of names) are recorded as attributes_status too_large without their
values and shown as a failed read; the rest of the scan is unaffected.
.TP
.B integrity.directories
true (default) records directory entries like any other path. false keeps
them out of the inventory: files, links and other entries inside are still
observed, but creating, removing or changing a directory produces no change
or report, and directory entries stored earlier are retired silently. A file
replaced by a directory is still reported as deleted. Useful for web trees,
where directories carry no evidence of tampering.
.TP
.B integrity.xattrs, integrity.acl
false (default). true adds extended attributes, including file capabilities
(security.capability) and security labels, or POSIX ACLs to the compared
fields; they are recorded either way, so selecting them needs no new
inventory. A change is coded P. They are compared only where both observations
could read them. doctor, and the daemon at start, check each source and nested
mount: a selected comparison that the filesystem does not support is reported
in their warnings, and the daemon logs it and keeps it in the status without
raising an error alert.
.TP
.B integrity.hash
algorithm is sha256 (default), sha512, sha512_256, sha3_256, sha3_512,
blake3 or xxhash64. on_event defaults true; full_scan_interval defaults 24h and
continues even if ordinary watch reconciliation is disabled.
full_scan_schedule and full_scan_timezone (UTC by default) provide a cron
alternative to full_scan_interval with the same inheritance and exclusivity
rules as watch.reconcile. The first inventory scan still requires full
hashing. Ordinary scans retain the existing metadata
and on_event policy; no second scanner or inventory is created. With on_event
false, a content edit that changes no other compared field is reported when
the next full scan hashes the file. Changing algorithm reports every regular
file as a content change when it is next hashed. xxhash64 is not
cryptographic: a deliberate collision can hide a content change.
.TP
.B notifications
use selects named notifiers; [] disables them. events selects change, error
and recovery. batch_window defaults 30s;
max_batch_window defaults 15m and must be at least batch_window. Change
notices for each repository and destination share a delivery window.
While gaps between notices do not exceed max_batch_window, completed windows
double up to that maximum;
setting both windows equal disables growth. Error and recovery notices keep the
base delay and remain separate. A restart resets only the volatile window size;
queued evidence remains in Pebble. max_pending defaults 10000.
Delivery retries persist. Each failed attempt doubles the retry delay, from
two seconds up to about 68 minutes, or longer when Slack asks for it. After 32
failed attempts, about a day, a
delivery stops retrying: it stays queued with its evidence without delaying
other deliveries or the Slack pace, is logged once as a warning, and is
counted in catalog.exhausted_notifications, which adds a status warning. A
daemon restart, which configuration and template changes require anyway,
retries it from the first attempt while its notifier is unchanged; outbox
clear discards it. Saturation increments
notification_overflow and records a warning. New notices are retained; the
watermark does not discard evidence. Backlogs can grow until delivery succeeds.
Changing/removing destinations defers old deliveries without redirecting them:
they stop retrying like any failed delivery, and a restart retries them only
once the original notifier is restored.
.TP
.B notifier files
Email: type: email, dsn: smtp://user:password@host:587?tls=starttls or
smtps://user:password@host:465, from and to (list). TLS is mandatory.
The optional DSN parameter tls_server_name sets the verified certificate name
when connecting through an IP address or a VPN alias. Certificate validation
remains enabled. Delivery errors distinguish missing STARTTLS, certificate
verification failures, hostname mismatches and timeouts without exposing
credentials or raw server responses. Configure TLS on the SMTP relay; a server
without STARTTLS is rejected and its notifications remain queued for retry.
Every email body, from any template or report, ends with a signature after a
"-- " delimiter line: "FSledger | " and the build version, the project slogan
and the project URL. Slack messages carry no signature.
Slack: type: slack and url containing an HTTPS webhook. Redirects are disabled.
Both may select a template by name. Templates have Host (server.name), Hostname
(kernel hostname when the message was queued), Repository, Event, ChangeID,
Paths,
Count, the per-kind counts Added, Modified and Deleted, Details (path, kind,
actor, observed time and field values) and
Reason
(error and recovery events only: the repository warnings behind the error, or
what the recovery recovered from). Message.Detailed reports whether every path
is listed with its fields, which holds up to Message.DetailLimit (250) paths.
Message.DisplayReason returns Reason with
control characters escaped, as the default templates print it: a reason can
quote mount points, source globs or path errors. The time
function formats Unix nanoseconds as UTC in RFC 3339; localtime formats them as
YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS ZONE in the server time zone, which the default templates
and reports use; duration renders a time span in days, hours or minutes.
Subject and body share a 16 MiB output limit. Email uses each field directly;
Slack sends the subject followed by the body and splits long output into parts.
Parts are posted one second apart and keep the webhook reserved until the last
one, so no other message the daemon sends to that webhook lands in between,
even from another repository.
The queue records each accepted part: when a later part fails, the retry, even
after a restart, resumes after the accepted parts while the message renders
identically.
Due change notices are summarized by repository, destination and
event. Each detail retains its change ID. One summary contains at most 2000
paths,
512 KiB of rendered email, or four Slack parts; excess complete deliveries
remain
queued for a later sender tick. A single larger delivery is sent intact so
evidence
is never truncated.
.PP
The default and example change templates list Code and Path before detailed
differences. Detail.Code is C (created), D (deleted), M (content/type/link
target), P (permissions or attributes only), MP (content and permissions) or T
(an mtime moved back before btime, with no other verified change). Permissions
are mode, UID and GID, and xattrs and acl when selected. An em dash indicates
insufficient evidence: a modification whose content could not be verified.
Detail.VisibleFields omits inode. A modification carries only the compared
fields that changed; a creation or deletion lists every recorded attribute of
the version that exists, leaving out target and hash when it has none.
Detail.DisplayFields renders those values for
people: timestamps with localtime, size in IEC units, mode as ls-style
permissions followed by the raw st_mode in hexadecimal (as stat -c %f prints
it), symlink targets as text, xattrs and ACLs as getfattr does (name="text", or
name=0x followed by hexadecimal for binary values such as ACLs) with their
readability status, and the absent side of a creation or deletion as an em
dash; control characters and invalid UTF-8 are escaped. They leave out
has_btime, device, nlink unless a regular file has more than one hard link,
and attributes_status unless reading xattrs and ACLs failed. Size, times and
attributes therefore appear only in creations and deletions, except a backdated
mtime and selected xattrs and acl.
Detail.BackdatedBy is how much earlier mtime is than btime for the version that
exists, the deleted one for a deletion (over one hour), a sign of times
preserved by a copy or of a backdated file. Creations, deletions and
modifications carry it alike; the default templates and reports print it as a
warning.
Detail.ActorLines returns the actor evidence as lines
without the detection method or the Git change trailer, with control
characters escaped; Detail.Detection returns the detection method and
Message.CommonDetection the method shared by every detail, or nothing when
they differ. Raw Fields and Actor remain available to custom
templates; stored evidence is unchanged. Existing custom templates must opt
into these methods. The default template states a shared detection method once;
above 250 paths it lists every path grouped by actor and change ID without
per-path fields, so one email carries up to 2000 paths.
Scheduled reports use the same list-first layout and display filtering.
.TP
.B exclude
Select exclusion profile names globally or per repository. Profile files contain
YAML lists of doublestar patterns. Global and repository profiles are additive.
An excluded directory also excludes its contents. A trailing slash, as in
gitignore, is dropped and repeated slashes collapse; patterns with . or ..
components never match clean paths and are rejected.
.SH EXAMPLE
Main configuration:
.nf
paths:
  repositories: [/etc/fsledger/services]
  notifiers: [/etc/fsledger/notifiers]
  templates: /etc/fsledger/templates
  excludes: [/etc/fsledger/excludes]
runtime: /run/fsledger
scan:
  workers: 2
  max_concurrent: 2
notifications:
  use: []
  batch_window: 30s
  max_batch_window: 15m
.fi
.PP
Contents of services/web.yaml:
.nf
type: db
paths: [/srv/www]
integrity:
  directories: false
.fi
.PP
Contents of templates/change.yaml:
.nf
subject: 'fsledger {{.Host}} / {{.Repository}}: {{.Event}} ({{.Count}})'
body: |
  Paths: {{.Count}} (+{{.Added}} ~{{.Modified}} -{{.Deleted}})
  {{if .ChangeID}}Change: {{.ChangeID}}{{end}}
.fi
.SH CONFIGURATION FORMAT
Configuration has no version field. Options evolve incrementally; only the
current format is supported and unknown fields are rejected. The installer
preserves existing configuration.
runtime is the only volatile-state location. All configuration changes require
restart; sources appearing or disappearing on disk do not.
With systemd, custom writable paths require a ReadWritePaths drop-in.
.SH NOTES
Git stores final contents and the executable bit, not full filesystem metadata
or every intermediate write. Source symlinks are preserved without following
them. Secrets may persist in Git history even after later exclusion.
Names Git refuses even on Linux, such as git~1, .git. or a symlink named
\&.gitmodules, stay in the catalog but are not copied to the mirror.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsledger (1),
.BR fsledgerd (8)

.SH HOST SUBSTITUTION AND GIT PUBLICATION
${HOST} and $HOST expand in YAML strings and keys using the HOST environment
variable, or the system hostname when unset. No shell commands are executed.
An explicitly empty HOST is rejected when a substitution is requested.
storage.git.remote enables optional publication to storage.git.branch (default
main).
An empty remote disables it. push_interval defaults to 1m and push_timeout to
30s.
remote accepts a configured Git remote name, an absolute local path, or an SSH
or
HTTPS URL; SCP-style user@host:path syntax is not accepted.
A path remote of a Git repository must lie outside storage and runtime state and
must not overlap any repository's sources: every push would otherwise change
what is watched. Pushes and fetches use the path its trusted links resolve to;
a link another user owns fails publication and fetching instead of steering them.
Credentials belong in SSH or Git credential configuration, never the remote URL.
Two Git repositories cannot publish to the same branch of one URL or path remote:
their histories are unrelated. Remote names are not compared.
Publication is serialized with repository work and never forces remote history.
Branch permissions restrict writes; read isolation requires separate
repositories.

.SH OPTIONAL BIDIRECTIONAL GIT
storage.git.bidirectional defaults to false. When true, a Git repository also
fetches and applies incoming commits from exactly storage.git.branch. A nonempty
remote is required; DB repositories reject this flag. fetch_interval defaults to
1m and fetch_timeout to 30s. All fields inherit individually and a repository
can explicitly set bidirectional to false.
.PP
Start by publishing the local snapshot, then edit a clone of that history using
paths relative to /. Incoming history must fast-forward the local HEAD. There is
no automatic merge, force push, checkout of sources or import of unrelated
history.
Pending local/deferred/unstable changes postpone import; divergent histories
need
manual resolution in a separate clone. Network errors do not block local
commits.
.PP
Enabling this flag grants remote branch writers the ability to modify selected
sources with daemon privileges. Use a trusted remote and branch protection.
Commit signatures are not verified. With the packaged systemd service, add only
authorized source directories to ReadWritePaths through an override; the default
ProtectSystem=strict deliberately prevents writes to arbitrary sources.
.PP
The complete change set is checked before mutation. Excluded paths, paths
outside
the current selection, .git, fsledger temporary names, submodules, hardlinks,
special permission bits,
file capabilities and file/directory transitions are rejected. Source symlinks
are never followed.
The limit is 4096 changed paths and 64 MiB per file. statx mount identity must
be
available. Empty directories are not removed.
.PP
Existing owners, ordinary permission bits and extended attributes are preserved
when supported; Git controls the executable bit. security.capability,
security.ima and security.evm belong to the replaced content and are never
copied. New entries belong to the
daemon
user; new files use 0600 (0700 when executable) and directories use 0700. Git
does
not supply ownership or extended attributes.
.PP
A durable Pebble journal holds object IDs and progress, not file contents. It is
recovered before ordinary local commits. Replacements use same-directory
temporaries, fsync and rename, with local-byte and root-identity revalidation.
There is no whole-tree atomicity or locking of external writers: coordinate
source writes during deployment. Partial imports are possible after
interruption.
I/O errors retain the journal for retry. Conflicts stop the import and allow
local
reconciliation to record actual state; there is no automatic rollback. A
conflicted
operation is not retried against the same target commit: review sources and
publish
a new resolution. The conflicted operation is discarded once the branch equals
the local history or falls behind it. Disabling bidirectional and restarting
cancels a pending
import
without reverting applied files.
.PP
fsledger status exposes remote_commit, last_fetched, last_fetch_error,
inbound_pending, inbound_applied, sync_blocked and sync_conflict.

.SH CHANGE FILTERS
notifications.ignore and commit.defer are lists of rules, globally or per
repository.
Omitting a list inherits it; an explicit list replaces it; [] disables
inheritance.
Each rule accepts users, command_regex and paths. Fields combine with AND;
values in each field and separate rules combine with OR. Paths use doublestar;
commands use Go regular expressions. Users are local names or uid:N, matched
against the observed process UID, never file ownership. Missing identity
evidence
cannot match identity rules. Empty rules and invalid expressions are rejected.
.PP
Process command lines from procfs or Audit are used only in memory for local
command_regex matching. Commits, catalog evidence and notifications omit process
arguments while retaining the observed user, PID and executable. Git failures
preserve exit status and cancellation without exposing command arguments or
hook output in status, logs or notifications.
.PP
notifications.ignore suppresses only change notifications. Inventory, errors
and recovery are unaffected.
commit.defer waits without a deadline until a non-deferred Git change or an
explicit
fsledger flush request. The initial snapshot is always committed. Only the
latest
observed version per path is retained; intermediate deferred versions are not
history and are not published. Pending decisions survive restart. Reconciliation
and shutdown respect deferral. A policy change reevaluates pending versions.
.PP
Storage directories for all loaded repositories and runtime are always
excluded. Source matches inside these directories are skipped and reported;
watching their ancestors is allowed. Independent daemons need shared exclusions
for each other's state.
Path components named .git and names starting with .fsledger-inbound- or
\&.fsledger-restore-, the temporaries fsledger itself creates in sources, are
always excluded as well.

.SH OPTIONAL AI COMMIT SUMMARIES
Named AI profiles are discovered in ai/ beside the canonical main configuration.
Profiles define provider (openai, openai-compatible or anthropic), endpoint,
model, api_key_env, timeout, max_diff_bytes and redact rules
(pattern/replacement).
No repository selection belongs in a profile.
endpoint and model are required. HTTPS is required except for HTTP on loopback
IP
addresses. Credentials are read from api_key_env. timeout defaults to 5s and
must
be positive and no greater than 5s. max_diff_bytes defaults to 64 KiB and
accepts
1 byte through 1 MiB.
.PP
A Git repository opts in with ia_commit: [profile] and ia_include:
[absolute-glob].
As in exclusions, repeated slashes collapse and patterns with . or .. components
are rejected. A pattern selects only the paths it matches, not the contents of
a matching directory; a trailing slash names a directory and selects everything
below it, as dir/** does.
Both lists must be nonempty to enable summaries; an empty selection leaves them
disabled. There is no global inheritance. Multiple profiles
are ordered alternatives. Their redaction rules are combined and applied to
complete path names and old and new blobs before creating a partial diff; a
name they cannot mask is omitted. Git bytes are unchanged.
.PP
Only regular UTF-8 text blobs without NUL bytes are eligible, with at most 64
KiB
per old or new blob. Each commit considers at most 32 selected paths and a
bounded
diff; binary files, symlinks and oversized blobs are omitted from AI input.
Five seconds total per summary and two simultaneous summaries per daemon are
allowed, shared with report AI. Busy capacity skips generation without waiting.
Failed providers have a one-minute cooldown within their session; later profiles
are attempted while the total deadline permits.
Provider failure never prevents a commit. Initial snapshots, deferred-only
changes
and shutdown do not request AI.

.SH SCHEDULED EMAIL REPORTS
reports fields inherit globally and per repository, including DB repositories.
Explicit lists replace inherited lists. enabled defaults to false; schedule to
"0 8 * * *"; timezone to UTC; use to []; send_empty to false.
format is detailed (default) or summary.
Use existing email notifier names in reports.use. Five-field cron expressions
are supported, without seconds or descriptors such as @daily. Timezone names
require installed IANA timezone data. Missing local times are skipped; repeated
local times can run twice at different UTC instants. Restart to apply changes.
.PP
Reports use a durable observation journal independent of immediate alerts.
Neither notifications.events nor notifications.ignore filters reports.
notifications.ignore suppresses only immediate change alerts, never report
observations. Only metadata observed while enabled
is
recorded; no historical reconstruction or source content storage occurs.
Periods include source observations, not necessarily successful Git commits.
Deferred flags describe the known state at observation; the last known HEAD
does not prove that a particular path version was committed.
.PP
Generation and SMTP run outside repository workers. With format=detailed a
frozen period is split into
numbered parts of at most 64 observations or approximately 16 KiB of serialized
metadata, except that individual observations are never truncated. Rendering is
limited to 16 MiB; oversized parts stay queued with an error. New observations
belong to the next period. Each part has its own optional AI introduction and
factual counts. Reports use a fixed plain-text format, not notifier templates.
.PP
With format=summary, a frozen period produces one logical message per
destination,
containing totals across all observations and a sample of at most 16 paths.
Aggregation reuses the metadata journal in durable chunks of 256 entries; it
does not rescan sources or load all observations into memory. Detailed evidence
remains in the journal until every destination acknowledges delivery, then is
removed in bounded transactions. Preview and failed delivery never consume it.
Optional AI summarizes the sample only; factual totals cover the complete
period.
An active scan at period close marks the report incomplete. The report schedule
is independent of scan schedules and does not wait for a scan to finish.
Set notifications.events to [error, recovery] to deliver changes only through
reports; this deliberately delays change alerts.
.PP
After downtime one accumulated period is prepared, not a separate message for
every missed schedule. Restart or incomplete observation sets a coverage
warning.
With send_empty false an empty period sends nothing even with that warning: the
warning is carried to the next report that has observations. Missing evidence is
never a guarantee that no changes occurred. Empty periods are omitted by
default.
Changing the schedule starts its next occurrence from reconfiguration time;
pending observations and already frozen periods are preserved.
.PP
Evidence moves atomically from journal to draft to durable outbox. Generated
text
and Message-ID are reused for SMTP retries. A crash after SMTP acceptance can
still cause duplicate delivery. A removed or changed notifier leaves its
existing
deliveries queued; they are never silently redirected. Disabled reporting
retains
pending journal/drafts; already queued messages still retry. There is no
retained
report archive after acknowledgement, nor automatic evidence-dropping retention.
Notification backlog remains a soft watermark, not a data-loss limit.
.PP
reports.ai.enabled defaults to false. When true, profiles and include must both
be nonempty. profiles references ordered alternatives from ai/. include takes
absolute patterns with the same rules as ia_include: a trailing slash selects
everything below a directory. input currently accepts only metadata (default).
Selection is independent of ia_commit and applies to Git and DB repositories.
Only selected paths, change kinds, field names and deferred flags are
sent, never source contents, attribute values or actor commands. The union of
profile redactions masks complete names before encoding and bounding the input.
At most 32 observations per part are sent, within the smallest selected
max_diff_bytes and never more than 64 KiB. AI failures produce deterministic text without delaying local commits;
the five-second deadline and two-request concurrency budget are shared with
commit summaries. SMTP retries do not call AI again.
.PP
fsledger status reports next, since, pending, last_queued, last_delivered and
coverage_gap under reports. Times are Unix nanoseconds; zero means unset.
pending
counts journal observations, not drafts or queued deliveries. last_delivered is
the last confirmed destination/part, not confirmation of an entire report.
For summary format, pending includes detailed evidence retained until all
destinations acknowledge and background cleanup completes.
fsledger report preview requires the daemon stopped and reads the first pending
part (or aggregates the complete summary period) without consuming evidence,
contacting AI or delivering mail.
